blocks.body:
A central angle has its vertex at the centre; its measure equals the measure of the arc it cuts off. An inscribed angle has its vertex on the circle; its measure is $\textbf{half}$ the arc it cuts off.

So if a central angle and an inscribed angle intercept the same arc, the inscribed angle is half the central angle. Two consequences the test loves:
\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
\item An angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle. If $\overline{AB}$ is a diameter and $C$ is on the circle, then $\angle ACB=90^\circ$.
\item A tangent line meets the radius drawn to the point of tangency at $90^\circ$, which turns tangent questions into right-triangle questions.
\end{itemize}

